The pattern, executed:

```go
func withHeader(next http.HandlerFunc) http.HandlerFunc {
    return func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        w.Header().Set("X-Custom", "yes")   // BEFORE — pre-processing
        next(w, r)                            // the actual handler
        // AFTER — post-processing (rare — response already sent)
    }
}

// wrap the handler
mux.HandleFunc("/mw", withHeader(myHandler))
// GET /mw → handler sees X-Custom: yes — verified
```

The shape: a function that RETURNS a function (the wrapper). The wrapper runs before the handler (pre: logging, auth, headers, request ID) and optionally after (post: response metrics — the response is already sent, so post is mostly cleanup).

The standard middleware chain — composable:

```go
func chain(h http.HandlerFunc, mw ...func(http.HandlerFunc) http.HandlerFunc) http.HandlerFunc {
    for i := len(mw) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {  // wrap in reverse
        h = mw[i](h)
    }
    return h
}
h := chain(handler, logging, auth, headers)
```

The common middlewares every service has: logging (method, path, status, duration), auth (check token, reject or attach to context), panic recovery (defer recover → 500), request ID (generate + set in context and response header).

The http.Handler interface variant: middleware can also be func(http.Handler) http.Handler — the more general form (works on muxes, not just handlers). The two forms compose; pick one per codebase.
